<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Chernigov</h1><p class="st-meta">1024 CE – 1239 CE (approx.) · capital Chernigov</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the largest and most powerful principalities of Kievan Rus', ruled by the Olgovichi, rivals of Kiev for the throne.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desna-river">Desna</a>, in the old Severian lands, became a princely seat in 1024 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-of-tmutarakan">Mstislav of Tmutarakan</a> made it his capital. Under the will of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> it passed in 1054 to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-ii-of-kiev">Sviatoslav II</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-liubech">Lyubech conference</a> of 1097 confirmed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav's</a> sons, founding the Olgovichi dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> to the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka</a> and was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novgorod-Seversky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a>. Its princes competed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and often allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transfiguration-cathedral-chernihiv">Transfiguration Cathedral</a> of the 1030s is among the oldest churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-igors-campaign">Lay of Igor's Campaign</a> celebrates one of its princes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> in 1239, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-chernigov">Prince Mikhail</a> was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu</a>'s camp in 1246. The land broke into small principalities under Mongol overlordship and in the 14th century passed largely to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-chernigov.jpg" alt="Principality of Chernigov" width="320" height="260"><figcaption>Image: SeikoEn перекладено Petriot333 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D0%B8%D1%97%D0%B2%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%A0%D1%83%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%9A%D0%BD%D1%8F%D0%B7%D1%96%D0%B2%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1024 CE – 1239 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chernigov</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mstislav of Tmutarakan, Sviatoslav II, Mikhail of Chernigov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine, Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Chernihiv</dd></dl></aside></div>
